   reg = rd.reg
  }

 This used to print the content to the console and return 0. A program
 that wrote a file and checked the result was told it had succeeded, and
 nothing had been written anywhere. Silent data loss, reported as
 success, in an operation whose entire purpose is not to lose data.

 There is no honest implementation to give it. Fat16 is the only
 filesystem the bare-metal target has and it is read-only -- it can
 resolve a path, list a directory and read a file, and it cannot create
 or write one. So the emitter refuses and says so, which is the rule: a
 target that cannot represent a feature does not get to pretend.

 What to use instead, until the write path exists: block-write-sector to
 put bytes on the disk, or write-binary to hand them back over the wire.
 Both say what they do.

 The phantoms went the other way about it and were no better. list-files,
 list-directories, create-directory and write-binary-file were bound in
 the type environment and named in builtin-names, and no emitter for any
 of them was ever written. The type checker vouched for the name and the
 build then died at the very end with CDX2040, a diagnostic about an
 unresolved function offset -- true, and no help at all to someone who
 had simply asked to list a directory. A name the compiler promises and
 cannot keep is worth less than no name, and it should say so at the
 first opportunity, in the words of the thing that was asked for.

 list-files and list-directories are no longer among them. They are real
 now, and they are not here: they live in Foreword chapter Fat16, next to
 file-exists and for the same reason -- the machine does not know what a
 directory is, and FAT16 does. So they are gone from builtin-names and
 from the type environment entirely, which is what lets a chapter that
 cites Fat16 resolve them to the definitions that work. A chapter that
 does not cite Fat16 now gets CDX3002 on the name, which is the truth: it
 asked for something it did not bring.

 write-file and write-binary-file are no longer here either. FAT16 is not
 read-only any more -- it allocates a cluster, chains it, writes the bytes
 and commits a directory entry, all in Codex over block-write-sector -- so
 both names now resolve to definitions that keep their promise, next to
 file-exists in Foreword chapter Fat16. The emitter has nothing to say about
 them. write-file used to live here and print its content to the console
 while reporting success, which is the strongest argument in this file for
 why a name the compiler cannot keep does not belong in the compiler.

 For a while after that, the string "write-file" lingered in
 sorted-builtin-names with no emitter behind it. That ghost was not
 harmless: an application site asks is-builtin BEFORE it consults the
 handler table, so a `with FileSystem` handler over write-file would
 discharge the row at type level and then die at emit looking for an
 emitter that does not exist. The name is out of the list now, which is
 what lets the FileSystem effect's write-file op reach a handler -- the
 layer that services it over the disk is the one place the name resolves.

 The paragraph above was true of write-file and false of write-binary-file,
 which stayed in sorted-builtin-names for months after this prose said it
 had gone -- routed by is-builtin, with no emitter and no NameResolver
 entry, so it answered CDX3002 to anyone who asked. list-append and
 list-cons sat the same way, and they had emitters, so all that kept them
 from reaching one was that the resolver had never heard of them either.
 None of the three had a single call site in the tree; cons and append
 reach __list_cons and __list_append through the :: and & operators
 (IrConsList / IrAppendList in X86_64.codex), which is why nothing ever
 noticed. All three are gone now (fester, 2026-07-16).

 A ghost is cheap to leave and expensive to find, and for a while this was
 checked by the build rather than by the next reader:
 build/check-builtin-tables.ps1 walked these lists and failed on a name
 that was routed with no emitter, or routed and unresolvable, or emitted
 and unrouted. A ghost of any of those shapes is now unrepresentable --
 one row carries the name, the type and the emitter, and routing is a
 projection of the emitter column -- so the script is deleted. So is
 check-cap-tables.ps1, which sat in the build for the same reason and
 lost it the same way when the capability table moved to the foreword.

 create-directory is gone too, and the sentence that used to sit here is the
 reason: it said the name stayed "because there is still nothing honest to
 point it at -- creating a directory means walking to a parent and writing the
 dot and dotdot entries, and that is not written." That was a condition, not a
 verdict, and it is met now. fat16-create-directory claims a cluster, zeroes
 it, writes the dot entries and commits the parent's entry, so the name
 resolves to something that keeps its promise and it lives where the promise
 is kept. The emitter has nothing to say about it either.

 That leaves this file with no name in it that the compiler cannot keep, which
 is the state build/check-builtin-tables.ps1 now holds it in.

 emit-no-target-impl stood here and is gone with them. It raised CDX2043 for
 a builtin the target could not perform, and nothing called it -- once the
 last phantom left this file there was no name to point it at, so the code
 refused a case that could no longer arise. A diagnostic no program can
 provoke is the same promise-that-is-not-kept the paragraph above forbids,
 and it is worse than the phantoms were: those at least failed loudly.
 emit-unknown-builtin raises CDX2042 for a name routed here with no emitter,
 which is the case that can still happen, and check-builtin-tables.ps1 fails
 the build before it does.

  emit-read-line-builtin : CodegenState -> EmitResult
  emit-read-line-builtin (st) =
    let st1 = emit-call-to st "__read_line"
   in let rd = alloc-temp st1
   in EmitResult {
   state = st-append-code (rd.state) (mov-rr (rd.reg) reg-rax),
   reg = rd.reg
  }

