Section: Builtin Registry
  BuiltinX86Emitter = record {
   name : Text,
   emit : CodegenState, List IRExpr -> EmitResult
  }

  bsearch-x86-emitter-pos : List BuiltinX86Emitter, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bsearch-x86-emitter-pos (entries) (name) (lo) (hi) =
   if lo >= hi then lo
   else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
   in let mid-name = (list-at entries mid).name
   in if text-compare name mid-name <= 0 then bsearch-x86-emitter-pos entries name lo mid
      else bsearch-x86-emitter-pos entries name (mid + 1) hi

  sort-x86-emitters : List BuiltinX86Emitter -> List BuiltinX86Emitter
  sort-x86-emitters (xs) = sort-by xs (\a b -> text-compare (a.name) (b.name))

 char-code, code-to-char and address-of are reinterpretations and not
 conversions: the operand is already the value the result wants, so all
 three emit the first argument and nothing else. They shared one inline
 lambda until the table moved to Types chapter Builtins, and that move is
 why this function exists rather than the lambda.

 emit-expr is defined twice in the unit -- here in the X86-64 emitter as
 CodegenState, IRExpr -> EmitResult, and in the Codex Emitter as IRExpr,
 List Text, Integer -> Text -- so which one a mention resolves to depends on
 the chapter the mention sits in. Written inline in the table it resolved to
 the text emitter and would not type-check. Written here it resolves to this
 one. The name is bound at the call site, so an emitter body is only
 portable between chapters if every name in it is unique across the unit;
 this is the single name in the whole table for which that is false. That is
 the collision, met head-on. A helper with an unambiguous name is the fix
 that does not depend on the collision either way.

  emit-identity-builtin : CodegenState, List IRExpr -> EmitResult
  emit-identity-builtin (st) (args) =
   emit-expr (st-set-tail-pos st False) (list-at args 0)

  emitters-from-builtins : List BuiltinSpec, Integer, Integer, List BuiltinX86Emitter -> List BuiltinX86Emitter
  emitters-from-builtins (bs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in when b.bs-emit
    is Just (f) -> emitters-from-builtins bs (i + 1) len (list-push acc (BuiltinX86Emitter { name = b.bs-name, emit = f }))
    is otherwise -> emitters-from-builtins bs (i + 1) len acc

  x86-builtin-emitters : List BuiltinX86Emitter
  x86-builtin-emitters = sort-x86-emitters (emitters-from-builtins builtins 0 (list-length builtins) [])

 This is the miss path, and it can no longer be reached from the routing
 list: sorted-builtin-names is derived from this very table, so a routed
 name has an emitter by construction. It stays because it is still the
 answer to a lookup that finds nothing, and because of what it replaced.
 The miss used to answer by emitting the constant 1 -- it fell through to
 the emitter for file-exists, whose whole body was `li rd, 1`. That is a
 silent miscompile: the builtin quietly becomes True. A miss that cannot
 happen must still be loud, because the cost of being wrong about that is
 a program that compiles and lies.

 That emitter is gone. file-exists never had an implementation worth the
 name -- it answered True for every path, existing or not -- and it is a
 real FAT16 lookup now, in Foreword chapter Fat16, where a filesystem
 question belongs. A program that asks it must cite the chapter, which is
 the loud failure this file argues for throughout: a name that resolves
 to nothing is better than a name that resolves to a lie.

  emit-unknown-builtin : CodegenState, Text -> EmitResult
  emit-unknown-builtin (st) (name) =
   let st0 = st-add-error st cdx-emit-not-implemented ("No x86-64 emitter for builtin '" & name & "'. Nothing routed here should be able to miss: sorted-builtin-names is derived from x86-builtin-emitters. Reaching this means emit-builtin was called for a name is-builtin did not admit.") synthetic-span
   in let st1 = emit-call-to st0 "__unresolved_trap"
   in let rd = alloc-temp st1
   in EmitResult {
    state = st-append-code (rd.state) (mov-rr (rd.reg) reg-rax),
    reg = rd.reg
   }

 The table is read out of the state, and that is load-bearing rather than
 tidy. x86-builtin-emitters is a zero-parameter definition, and a mention of
 one is emitted as a call, not read from a slot: this function once made
 three mentions and so built and sorted all 143 entries three times over on
 every builtin emission in the program. Binding it to a local cut that to
 one, which was the whole fix at the time.

 One per emission is now too many. The emitters are a projection of the
 builtins table, so building them means building all 244 rows and every
 type expression they carry, and doing that per emission would be far worse
 than the three-fold waste this paragraph used to describe. So it is built
 where sorted-builtin-names is built -- once, where a CodegenState is
 constructed -- and carried forward from function to function as a pointer.
 The search below is unchanged.

  emit-builtin : CodegenState, Text, List IRExpr -> EmitResult
  emit-builtin (st) (name) (args) =
   let es = st.builtin-emitters
   in let len = list-length es
   in let pos = bsearch-x86-emitter-pos es name 0 len
   in if pos >= len then emit-unknown-builtin st name
      else let e = list-at es pos
      in if e.name == name then (e.emit) st args
         else emit-unknown-builtin st name


